 "intro_html": "The everyday stew that comes with rice at a Korean table: deeply savoury from fermented soybean paste, not spicy unless you add the chilli. Cooked in a <b>wide steel sauté pan with a lid</b> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"26\" data-b=\"28\" data-s=\"0\">26–28 cm</span>, sides <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"5\" data-s=\"0\">5 cm</span> or more) instead of a clay pot. Stainless steel is fine with salty, fermented food. The lid stays on for most of the cooking because a wide pan loses liquid quickly. Use <a href=\"niboshi-bonito-dashi.html\">niboshi dashi</a> if you have it. Shrimp, clams or cubes of beef are optional; vegetables and tofu alone make a complete stew. This is a home adaptation built from several Korean sources and has not been kitchen tested.",
