 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A jjigae is a shallow stew, so a wide pan with high sides does the job of a clay pot.</li><li>The difference is evaporation: keep the lid on until the tofu goes in, and keep a cup of hot water nearby.</li><li>Stainless steel is fine with fermented paste.</li><li>A carbon-steel pan would lose its seasoning.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why a steel pan works"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Saltiness differs a lot between brands, so start at <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"2.5\" data-s=\"0\">2½ tbsp</span> and add more at the end.</li><li>Doenjang is the brown Korean paste.</li><li>Japanese miso works in a pinch but tastes sweeter and milder; use a little more and add <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"0.5\" data-s=\"0\">½ tsp</span> soy sauce.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Doenjang brands vary"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Simmer 6 dried anchovies (heads and guts removed) and a <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"5\" data-s=\"0\">5 cm</span> piece of kombu in <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"750\" data-s=\"0\">750 ml</span> water for 10 minutes, then lift them out.</li><li>Plain water with an extra spoon of doenjang also works.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "No dashi?"
  }
 ],
